    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09491v1</id>
    <title>An Effective Spoken Language Understanding Approach Leveraging Query-Adaptive Intent Detection</title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T14:13:17Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09491v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.09491v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language models enable fine-tuning-free spoken language understanding (SLU) through in-context learning, but direct query-based retrieval may select semantically similar yet intent irrelevant demonstrations. This paper presents Query-Adaptive Intent Detection via Prototype Extraction (APEX), a two-stage retrieval framework that first selects candidate intents using intent-level prototypes and then retrieves contextual exemplars within the selected intent space. Experiments on the SLURP dataset evaluate joint SLU performance using Exact Match Accuracy (EMA) under full-transcript and perplexity-stratified conditions, with additional retrieval analysis on ASR-generated transcripts. Compared with query-based retrieval, APEX improves EMA by 4.34 and 3.97 percentage points with Qwen3 4B and Gemma-3-4B, respectively, on full transcripts. Under high-perplexity inputs, APEX also yields EMA gains of 7.66 and 2.13 percentage points. Retrieval analysis further indicates that prototype-guided candidate selection achieves higher ground truth intent coverage across multiple input conditions.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-06T14:13:17Z</published>
