    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09396v1</id>
    <title>Travel Cost Calibration for Multi-Agent Target Assignment and Pathfinding</title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T12:34:10Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09396v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.09396v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Target assignment and pathfinding (TAPF) is an appealing extension of classical multi-agent pathfinding (MAPF): each agent has a set of targets, and the planner must both assign a target to each agent and plan collision-free paths for them. A trivial approach is to compute an assignment that minimizes the total single-agent shortest-path distance over agent--target pairs, and then solve the resulting MAPF instance. However, this approach falls short because the shortest-path distance remains a proxy that ignores interference among agents. Assigning targets based on the costs agents actually pay, which depend on how every other agent is assigned and routed, would yield a better final outcome. To this end, we propose an iterative TAPF method that improves target assignments by calibrating travel cost estimates from fast, suboptimal MAPF feedback. Experiments show that our method produces solutions close to optimal on small instances and outperforms existing planners in solution quality on larger ones.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-06T12:34:10Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yu Kumagai</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ryohei Banno</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Keisuke Okumura</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09385v1</id>
    <title>Against All Likelihood</title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T12:23:43Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09385v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.09385v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Maximum likelihood is the dominant training principle for large language models, yet likelihood is neither necessary nor sufficient for truth. Unlikely events happen every day, and frequently repeated statements can be false. Drawing on a philosophical tradition that ties objectivity to invariance rather than probability (from Nozick's account of objective facts and Wigner's principles of invariance to the structural realism of Poincaré and Cassirer), we argue that the truth of a statement should be judged not by its probability under a single distribution, but by its invariance across relevant transformations of the environment. We connect this view to machine learning literature on out-of-distribution generalization and invariant learning, sketch how invariance could be tested at inference time with language models, and discuss implications for continual learning.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-06T12:23:43Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>David Lopez-Paz</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09382v1</id>
    <title>Exploring Weight-Space Programming: Capabilities, Generalization, and Limitations</title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T12:22:24Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09382v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.09382v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Weight-Space Programming (WSP) enables reasoning agents to directly inspect and modify neural network parameters, offering an alternative to gradient-based model adaptation. However, its capabilities, cross-task generalization, and practical limitations remain insufficiently understood. We systematically investigate WSP through 16 editing trajectories across seven benchmarks and two model scales (Qwen3.5-0.8B and Qwen3.5-9B). Our findings reveal three key insights. First, WSP can achieve substantial performance gains through highly localized interventions. On full held-out evaluations, accuracy improves by up to 29.45, 4.42, 36.72, and 17.11 percentage points on MATH-500, GPQA Diamond, IFEval, and SciBench, respectively. Notably, modifying only 1,023 parameters yields a 29.45-point improvement. Second, WSP exhibits promising but uneven cross-task generalization. A Math-edited checkpoint improves GPQA Diamond, IFEval, and SciBench by 3.03, 12.18, and 20.10 points, respectively, without further editing, while leaving SciCode performance unchanged. Third, effective editing remains challenging and inconsistent. Outcomes vary substantially across trajectories, and six of the 16 trajectories retain the original weights when candidate improvements cannot be reliably validated. Together, these results highlight WSP's potential for highly localized model adaptation while revealing important challenges in intervention discovery, reliability, and cross-task transfer.
  Homepage: https://wsp-scope.github.io/ GitHub: https://github.com/WSP-Scope/WSP-Scope-Project</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
